    15	#      1. matching: the total number of non-background 4-neighbors at the anchor
    16	#         must equal the legend source's count, so rules are keyed by
    17	#         (source_color, anchor_neighbor_count);
    18	#      2. recoloring: among those same non-background neighbors, the unique most
    19	#         frequent color becomes the stamp color for that particular block.
    20	#    - To make this visible and stable, every source block is chosen from a shape
    21	#      family whose top-left anchor has exactly one internal same-color neighbor.
    22	#      The remaining anchor neighbors are external colored support pixels.
    23	#      Count classes are therefore only 3 or 4:
    24	#      * count 3 = {1 internal source neighbor, 2 support pixels of one color}
    25	#      * count 4 = {1 internal source neighbor, 2 support pixels of winner color,
    26	#                   1 support pixel of a minority color}
    27	#    - The legend mirrors that structure. Because the legend source is only one
    28	#      pixel, we add one same-color helper next to it to stand in for the block's
    29	#      internal neighbor. The guide pixel is one of the winner-colored neighbors,
    30	#      and optional extra support pixels complete count 3 or 4.
    31	#    - Trace example:
    32	#      a red rule of count 3 has legend neighborhood {red helper, yellow guide,
    33	#      yellow support}. A free red domino whose top-left cell touches two yellow
    34	#      supports also has count 3, so it matches and stamps its teal stencil in
    35	#      yellow. Another red block with neighborhood {red internal, green, green,
    36	#      maroon} has count 4 instead, so it matches the red count-4 rule and stamps
    37	#      in green. The original task would have ignored both the block geometry and
    38	#      the neighborhood split.
    39	#
    40	# b) Complexity calibration
    41	#    - Neighbor counts are restricted to {3, 4}. This is small enough for humans
    42	#      to read but still enough to split one source color into two rules.
    43	#    - Source colors come from a tiny palette, and the generator always creates at
    44	#      least one repeated source color across different counts. That makes the
    45	#      extra match condition necessary rather than decorative.
    46	#    - Vote colors come from a separate small palette and at least one rule is
    47	#      forced to use two different majority colors across its source blocks, so
    48	#      the per-instance recolor cannot collapse back to a fixed target color.
    49	#
    50	# c) Hidden assumptions that break in the original solver
    51	#    - Iterating over every pixel of a source color is no longer valid: one block
    52	#      must produce one stamp, not one stamp per cell.
    53	#    - Source color alone no longer determines a rule, so rule discovery must
    54	#      store the legend source's anchor-neighbor count as part of the key.
    55	#    - A fixed legend target color is no longer meaningful. The guide pixel still
    56	#      helps locate the stencil, but the actual output color must be recomputed
    57	#      from each block anchor's neighborhood.
    58	#    - Legend-local helper/support pixels now exist. If they are not removed from
    59	#      consideration, the solver would mistake them for free-standing sources or
    60	#      preserve them in the output.
    61	#
    62	# d) Placement legality
    63	#    - Legends, source blocks, support pixels, bystanders, and teal stencils are
    64	#      all placed on disjoint footprints with a reserved 4-neighborhood margin.
    65	#      This prevents accidental merges, unstable counts, and false local rules.
